WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.961
<v Alex>Here is a small story. One person changes a rule for his A I assistants. Twice.

2
00:00:06.961 --> 00:00:08.267
<v Alex>In one morning.

3
00:00:08.842 --> 00:00:13.009
<v Maya>And each time, he adds the new rule to the memory file.

4
00:00:13.638 --> 00:00:15.638
<v Alex>But he never deletes the old one.

5
00:00:16.243 --> 00:00:19.349
<v Maya>So now the file says two different things.

6
00:00:19.349 --> 00:00:23.232
<v Maya>And the assistant just believes whichever line it reads first.

7
00:00:23.727 --> 00:00:27.946
<v Alex>Today we talk about why stale memory is worse than no memory.

8
00:00:28.846 --> 00:00:35.186
<v Alex>Welcome to The Daily Signal. Your daily download on A I, growth, and building things.

9
00:00:35.796 --> 00:00:36.964
<v Alex>I'm Alex.

10
00:00:37.589 --> 00:00:43.164
<v Maya>And I'm Maya. It's Friday, October sixteenth, twenty twenty-six.

11
00:00:43.615 --> 00:00:47.024
<v Alex>Quick note first. Today's episode is a little different.

12
00:00:47.024 --> 00:00:52.477
<v Alex>No music today, just the two of us. And it is a bit shorter than usual.

13
00:00:53.091 --> 00:00:57.279
<v Maya>Today's notes come from Fisher's X home feed.

14
00:00:57.279 --> 00:01:01.885
<v Maya>Only the Following feed, read once, early this morning, Beijing time.

15
00:01:01.885 --> 00:01:06.073
<v Maya>So some posts from yesterday daytime are not in here.

16
00:01:06.682 --> 00:01:12.663
<v Alex>Here's what's coming today. One. Agent memory goes stale, and what to do about it.

17
00:01:13.206 --> 00:01:17.933
<v Maya>Two. Using A I to protect the bar, not to make more stuff.

18
00:01:18.444 --> 00:01:22.415
<v Alex>Three. Why so many A I-built websites look the same.

19
00:01:22.772 --> 00:01:29.435
<v Maya>简单说：今天讲记忆过期、守住标准、还有长得一样的网站。

20
00:01:30.435 --> 00:01:32.502
<v Alex>Segment one. Top stories.

21
00:01:33.002 --> 00:01:35.123
<v Maya>First post is from klöss.

22
00:01:35.123 --> 00:01:39.719
<v Maya>That's a builder account on X with about seventy-nine thousand followers.

23
00:01:40.258 --> 00:01:44.491
<v Alex>His title is: Your agent's memory is lying to you.

24
00:01:45.042 --> 00:01:47.747
<v Maya>Strong title. What does he mean?

25
00:01:48.307 --> 00:01:54.395
<v Alex>Memory files and rule files get old. They go stale. But the agent reads them as fact.

26
00:01:54.395 --> 00:01:57.259
<v Alex>And it acts on them with full confidence.

27
00:01:57.908 --> 00:02:01.685
<v Maya>So it never says, hmm, maybe this is outdated?

28
00:02:02.293 --> 00:02:06.930
<v Alex>No. It just does it. His fix is a monthly audit.

29
00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:14.097
<v Alex>You ask the agent to read every memory and rule file. Then flag three kinds of lines.

30
00:02:14.498 --> 00:02:16.337
<v Maya>Okay. Which three?

31
00:02:16.985 --> 00:02:21.419
<v Alex>Lines that contradict another file. Lines that are probably outdated.

32
00:02:21.419 --> 00:02:25.410
<v Alex>And lines written as fact that you never confirmed.

33
00:02:25.903 --> 00:02:27.246
<v Maya>And then it fixes them?

34
00:02:27.728 --> 00:02:30.163
<v Alex>No. That's the key part.

35
00:02:30.163 --> 00:02:36.252
<v Alex>For each line, it quotes it, explains why it looks wrong, and suggests a fix.

36
00:02:36.252 --> 00:02:39.093
<v Alex>But it changes nothing until you approve.

37
00:02:39.493 --> 00:02:45.483
<v Maya>也就是说：让 A I 找出过期的规则，但你批准前它不能改。

38
00:02:45.942 --> 00:02:51.431
<v Alex>His last line is the one I like. Stale memory is worse than no memory.

39
00:02:51.431 --> 00:02:54.724
<v Alex>At least with no memory, the agent asks you.

40
00:02:55.624 --> 00:02:59.429
<v Maya>And this is not just theory for Fisher. It happened this week.

41
00:02:59.886 --> 00:03:00.364
<v Alex>Right.

42
00:03:00.364 --> 00:03:06.602
<v Alex>On October fifteenth, the storage plan for his project changed twice in one morning.

43
00:03:06.602 --> 00:03:11.058
<v Alex>First, the cloud computer was the one source of truth.

44
00:03:11.058 --> 00:03:15.960
<v Alex>Then, about forty-five minutes later, it moved to his laptop.

45
00:03:16.512 --> 00:03:19.855
<v Maya>And the old line is still in the memory file.

46
00:03:19.855 --> 00:03:24.200
<v Maya>The README also still says the cloud computer is the source of truth.

47
00:03:24.744 --> 00:03:29.182
<v Alex>So a new assistant could read the old line first and trust it.

48
00:03:29.815 --> 00:03:37.163
<v Maya>Our daily suggests one thing. Ask the Owner bot to clean out the old storage lines.

49
00:03:37.163 --> 00:03:39.919
<v Maya>But Fisher decides. Nothing was changed.

50
00:03:40.494 --> 00:03:43.105
<v Alex>And this is also today's original post idea.

51
00:03:43.105 --> 00:03:46.005
<v Alex>We'll come back to that in the content segment.

52
00:03:46.557 --> 00:03:50.757
<v Maya>So the takeaway is: when you change a rule, delete the old line.

53
00:03:50.757 --> 00:03:53.665
<v Maya>Don't just add a new one below it.

54
00:03:54.914 --> 00:03:58.922
<v Maya>一句话：改规矩时，把旧的那行删掉。

55
00:04:01.772 --> 00:04:08.210
<v Alex>Next, Eric Siu. He runs a marketing agency. About forty-six thousand followers.

56
00:04:08.710 --> 00:04:13.902
<v Maya>His post says: I use A I to protect the bar more than to produce more.

57
00:04:14.354 --> 00:04:17.334
<v Alex>Protect the bar. Meaning keep the standard high.

58
00:04:17.822 --> 00:04:23.711
<v Maya>He gives three bots as examples. First, an outreach bot. It has a daily limit.

59
00:04:23.711 --> 00:04:29.207
<v Maya>If it can't find enough good people to contact, it sends fewer emails.

60
00:04:29.795 --> 00:04:32.636
<v Alex>Instead of lowering the standard to hit a number.

61
00:04:33.126 --> 00:04:35.508
<v Maya>Exactly. Second, a hiring bot.

62
00:04:35.508 --> 00:04:39.796
<v Maya>It scores every applicant against a frozen role description.

63
00:04:39.796 --> 00:04:45.514
<v Maya>So the first candidate and the last candidate get the same ruler.

64
00:04:45.882 --> 00:04:46.346
<v Alex>Third?

65
00:04:46.797 --> 00:04:47.955
<v Maya>A sponsorship bot.

66
00:04:47.955 --> 00:04:54.516
<v Maya>When someone asks for a price, the first reply is fixed, and there is a minimum price.

67
00:04:54.516 --> 00:04:56.446
<v Maya>Exceptions go to a human.

68
00:04:57.062 --> 00:05:02.439
<v Alex>So a person makes the decision once. Then the bot applies it the same way every day.

69
00:05:02.779 --> 00:05:10.088
<v Maya>简单说：人先定好标准，Bot 每天照着执行，不为凑数降低标准。

70
00:05:10.592 --> 00:05:12.955
<v Alex>He had a second post the same day.

71
00:05:12.955 --> 00:05:17.092
<v Alex>He says if only you can use a bot, you just made yourself faster.

72
00:05:17.092 --> 00:05:21.525
<v Alex>If someone else on the team can use it, you change how the department works.

73
00:05:22.151 --> 00:05:28.027
<v Maya>This matches Fisher's own rules. His home feed is read only once a day.

74
00:05:28.027 --> 00:05:33.512
<v Maya>Stop near the budget limit. And every draft waits for him to click send.

75
00:05:34.064 --> 00:05:37.706
<v Alex>Fisher replied to Eric's read-only bot post yesterday.

76
00:05:37.706 --> 00:05:40.984
<v Alex>So this one is a natural follow-up reply.

77
00:05:41.603 --> 00:05:47.575
<v Maya>So the takeaway is: most good limits for A I are about doing less, not more.

78
00:05:48.853 --> 00:05:53.076
<v Maya>一句话：给 A I 的规矩，大多是让它少做。

79
00:05:55.926 --> 00:05:58.445
<v Alex>Segment two. Building and design.

80
00:05:59.044 --> 00:06:05.410
<v Maya>This one is from Shann Holmberg, based in Lisbon. About thirty-seven thousand followers.

81
00:06:05.879 --> 00:06:10.204
<v Alex>The topic: why so many A I-built websites look the same.

82
00:06:10.763 --> 00:06:16.920
<v Maya>Because everyone uses the same model. And the model has default design choices.

83
00:06:17.306 --> 00:06:17.860
<v Alex>Like what?

84
00:06:18.484 --> 00:06:25.159
<v Maya>The same headline style. The same hover effects. Text that animates word by word.

85
00:06:25.681 --> 00:06:30.420
<v Alex>Ah. I've seen those. Cards that lift up when your mouse goes over them.

86
00:06:30.989 --> 00:06:37.745
<v Maya>Yes, and for no reason. So your site starts to look familiar. Not because of your brand.

87
00:06:37.745 --> 00:06:39.732
<v Maya>Just because of the model.

88
00:06:40.194 --> 00:06:42.631
<v Alex>Can't you just tell it, don't look like A I?

89
00:06:43.158 --> 00:06:49.707
<v Maya>He says that's not enough. He points to Anthropic's guide for Opus five point five.

90
00:06:49.707 --> 00:06:54.714
<v Maya>Saying avoid the generic look can just swap one default style for another.

91
00:06:55.076 --> 00:06:55.975
<v Alex>So what works?

92
00:06:56.455 --> 00:07:02.009
<v Maya>Name the exact patterns you don't want. Italic accent words in every headline.

93
00:07:02.009 --> 00:07:06.373
<v Maya>Cards that lift on hover. Then give reference sites you like.

94
00:07:06.373 --> 00:07:13.911
<v Maya>And save those decisions in a brand book, so you don't fix the same thing on every project.

95
00:07:14.624 --> 00:07:21.352
<v Maya>也就是说：别笼统说“不要 A I 感”，要点名具体不要哪几种样式。

96
00:07:21.878 --> 00:07:23.620
<v Alex>Why does this matter for Fisher?

97
00:07:24.266 --> 00:07:29.686
<v Maya>He doesn't write code. His websites are built with A I.

98
00:07:29.686 --> 00:07:32.847
<v Maya>YouChina, Burn four fifty-one, Aluminium China.

99
00:07:33.415 --> 00:07:35.961
<v Alex>And he already has the same rule for writing.

100
00:07:36.532 --> 00:07:40.967
<v Maya>Right. "Don't sound like A I" doesn't work well.

101
00:07:40.967 --> 00:07:46.612
<v Maya>A list of exact phrases to avoid works better. Same idea, now for design.

102
00:07:47.189 --> 00:07:53.342
<v Alex>Writing idea? Only if he has real screenshots where his own sites got the same style.

103
00:07:53.342 --> 00:07:55.649
<v Alex>Without that, the daily says skip.

104
00:07:56.235 --> 00:08:02.852
<v Maya>So the takeaway is: be specific about what you don't want, and save it so you don't repeat the fix.

105
00:08:04.032 --> 00:08:09.609
<v Maya>一句话：把“不要什么”写具体，并存进品牌手册。

106
00:08:12.459 --> 00:08:18.409
<v Alex>Related post. From an account called staysaasy, about seventeen thousand followers.

107
00:08:18.409 --> 00:08:20.573
<v Alex>They write about management.

108
00:08:21.111 --> 00:08:25.599
<v Maya>The story is about interns at small startups in the twenty-tens.

109
00:08:26.097 --> 00:08:28.406
<v Alex>The summer interns. I remember.

110
00:08:28.937 --> 00:08:34.493
<v Maya>They got tasks like, make the analytics page cooler. But nobody said what cooler meant.

111
00:08:34.493 --> 00:08:39.309
<v Maya>Or where the page was. Or how to know when it's done.

112
00:08:39.641 --> 00:08:41.084
<v Alex>Ha. Classic.

113
00:08:41.728 --> 00:08:46.197
<v Maya>Their point: a coding agent that nobody guides well is like those interns.

114
00:08:46.197 --> 00:08:49.978
<v Maya>But they never go home at the end of the summer.

115
00:08:50.326 --> 00:08:51.414
<v Alex>That's a little scary.

116
00:08:51.998 --> 00:08:55.214
<v Maya>And all the old ways of managing interns still apply.

117
00:08:55.824 --> 00:09:03.202
<v Maya>简单说：管 A I 跟管新人一样，要讲清目标、位置和怎么算做完。

118
00:09:03.827 --> 00:09:12.287
<v Alex>Fisher managed people for years. His team rules already have handover fields. Goal. Owner.

119
00:09:12.287 --> 00:09:14.703
<v Alex>Deliverable. Next step. Status.

120
00:09:15.286 --> 00:09:23.485
<v Maya>So the content idea is: I write tasks for A I the same way I used to write handover notes for new people.

121
00:09:23.485 --> 00:09:24.911
<v Maya>Good for next week.

122
00:09:25.387 --> 00:09:30.033
<v Alex>So the takeaway is: a clear handover note works for agents too.

123
00:09:31.062 --> 00:09:35.423
<v Maya>一句话：给 A I 写任务，就是写交接单。

124
00:09:38.273 --> 00:09:41.728
<v Alex>Quick one. Ben Tossell asks a simple question.

125
00:09:41.728 --> 00:09:47.343
<v Alex>With personal agents, will we end up with one thread, or many agents?

126
00:09:47.982 --> 00:09:51.990
<v Maya>About two hundred thousand followers. Lots of replies.

127
00:09:52.621 --> 00:09:58.716
<v Alex>Fisher is in the many-agents group. One for personal brand. One for income opportunities.

128
00:09:58.716 --> 00:10:01.966
<v Alex>And an Owner bot that checks the others.

129
00:10:02.530 --> 00:10:06.740
<v Maya>So he can answer from real use. Not just pick a side.

130
00:10:07.219 --> 00:10:11.296
<v Alex>So the takeaway is: the hard part isn't how many agents.

131
00:10:11.296 --> 00:10:15.373
<v Alex>It's making sure two of them never edit the same file.

132
00:10:16.318 --> 00:10:22.757
<v Maya>一句话：难的不是几个助手，而是别让两个助手改同一份文件。

133
00:10:25.607 --> 00:10:26.437
<v Maya>Quick break.

134
00:10:26.437 --> 00:10:37.631
<v Maya>When we come back: an A I tracking problem that fixed itself, a voice tip from Greg Isenberg, and the question of whether websites should become chats.

135
00:10:38.367 --> 00:10:40.862
<v Maya>休息一下，马上回来。

136
00:10:42.362 --> 00:10:44.965
<v Alex>Segment three. Growth and search.

137
00:10:45.526 --> 00:10:50.673
<v Maya>Lily Ray. She's one of the best-known S E O experts.

138
00:10:50.673 --> 00:10:55.821
<v Maya>S E O means search engine optimization, how you get found on Google.

139
00:10:56.307 --> 00:10:59.214
<v Alex>About one hundred twenty-four thousand followers.

140
00:10:59.841 --> 00:11:07.608
<v Maya>A few days ago, on the October fourteenth daily, we said links from ChatGPT stopped carrying U T M tags.

141
00:11:08.186 --> 00:11:11.856
<v Alex>U T M tags are just small labels in a link.

142
00:11:11.856 --> 00:11:14.859
<v Alex>They tell your analytics where a visitor came from.

143
00:11:15.422 --> 00:11:22.073
<v Maya>Without them, A I traffic can show up as Direct. Like the person typed your address.

144
00:11:22.442 --> 00:11:23.079
<v Alex>And now?

145
00:11:23.611 --> 00:11:29.770
<v Maya>Lily Ray says the issue is already patched. She credits another account for the tip.

146
00:11:30.121 --> 00:11:31.889
<v Alex>Oh. That was fast.

147
00:11:32.347 --> 00:11:40.268
<v Maya>In a second post, she says Google's new features are things S E O people asked for for years.

148
00:11:40.268 --> 00:11:42.645
<v Maya>But people still need to click.

149
00:11:42.645 --> 00:11:47.398
<v Maya>She also wants more exact A I tracking in Google Search Console.

150
00:11:48.123 --> 00:11:53.962
<v Maya>也就是说：ChatGPT 链接不带来源标签的问题已经修好了。

151
00:11:54.506 --> 00:12:02.865
<v Alex>This changes one of Fisher's topic ideas, W M P two b. It was about losing U T M tags.

152
00:12:03.425 --> 00:12:10.727
<v Maya>So the angle needs to change. Maybe: don't trust only one data source for A I traffic.

153
00:12:10.727 --> 00:12:14.784
<v Maya>The tags went away and came back in one week.

154
00:12:15.299 --> 00:12:19.253
<v Alex>His evidence item E V oh two still works.

155
00:12:19.253 --> 00:12:26.723
<v Alex>Zero ads, and A I assistants are a top source of visits. No absolute numbers in public.

156
00:12:27.323 --> 00:12:31.963
<v Maya>So the takeaway is: check A I traffic from more than one place.

157
00:12:33.118 --> 00:12:37.442
<v Maya>一句话：看 A I 流量别只靠一个口径。

158
00:12:40.292 --> 00:12:47.925
<v Alex>Brodie Clark, an S E O consultant in Australia. He says rank tracking is broken right now.

159
00:12:48.449 --> 00:12:53.037
<v Maya>Rank tracking. Checking where your page shows up in Google for a keyword.

160
00:12:53.493 --> 00:12:58.199
<v Alex>He says poisoned results pages hit almost every data provider.

161
00:12:58.199 --> 00:13:05.259
<v Alex>His workaround: connect a tool called SERP Lens to Claude Desktop through M C P.

162
00:13:05.734 --> 00:13:09.955
<v Maya>M C P is a way to plug a tool into an A I assistant.

163
00:13:10.598 --> 00:13:14.859
<v Alex>Then Claude checks his client's keywords every morning from Sydney.

164
00:13:14.859 --> 00:13:18.345
<v Alex>And logs the rank and the page into Notion.

165
00:13:18.926 --> 00:13:25.912
<v Maya>To be fair, the post ends with "comment SERP for the guide". So it's partly marketing.

166
00:13:25.912 --> 00:13:28.789
<v Maya>And the results are his own claim.

167
00:13:29.325 --> 00:13:35.779
<v Alex>For Fisher, it's the right style of tool. Plug something in, let A I check every day.

168
00:13:35.779 --> 00:13:38.288
<v Alex>Maybe try once for YouChina, without paying.

169
00:13:38.843 --> 00:13:44.029
<v Maya>So the takeaway is: interesting, but not tested by anyone else yet.

170
00:13:45.354 --> 00:13:50.305
<v Maya>一句话：可以试，但效果是作者自己说的。

171
00:13:53.155 --> 00:13:59.519
<v Alex>Greg Isenberg. Big account, about seven hundred thirty-eight thousand followers.

172
00:14:00.038 --> 00:14:06.854
<v Maya>He posted ten insights from a week with Gemini four Argon. Important: it's sponsored.

173
00:14:06.854 --> 00:14:11.397
<v Maya>He says Google gave early access and sponsored the episode.

174
00:14:11.965 --> 00:14:14.339
<v Alex>Good that he says it. Which points matter?

175
00:14:14.926 --> 00:14:19.442
<v Maya>Two. First, without a reference image, his design looked bad.

176
00:14:19.442 --> 00:14:23.507
<v Maya>With a Mondrian painting as reference, it looked beautiful.

177
00:14:24.028 --> 00:14:27.027
<v Alex>References again. Same as the website story.

178
00:14:27.581 --> 00:14:33.307
<v Maya>Second, he asked it to read everything he ever wrote, then make a voice guide.

179
00:14:33.307 --> 00:14:34.834
<v Maya>His words, his templates.

180
00:14:35.437 --> 00:14:38.055
<v Alex>And Fisher does this a bit differently.

181
00:14:38.686 --> 00:14:44.425
<v Maya>Yes. Fisher's voice source is only his articles from before twenty twenty-five.

182
00:14:44.425 --> 00:14:48.523
<v Maya>Because newer posts were already written with A I help.

183
00:14:49.003 --> 00:14:52.388
<v Alex>So if you feed those in, it learns to sound like A I.

184
00:14:53.025 --> 00:14:57.291
<v Maya>Exactly. That's a detail only someone who did it would know.

185
00:14:57.842 --> 00:15:04.381
<v Maya>简单说：让 A I 学口吻时，别给它看已经有 A I 味的新文章。

186
00:15:04.982 --> 00:15:09.746
<v Alex>So the takeaway is: choose which of your writing the model learns from.

187
00:15:10.772 --> 00:15:14.019
<v Maya>一句话：喂给 A I 的样本要挑。

188
00:15:16.869 --> 00:15:23.125
<v Alex>Now, three posts that fit together. Paul Mit shares Blandie, from a company called Bland.

189
00:15:23.125 --> 00:15:25.627
<v Alex>You give it any business website.

190
00:15:25.627 --> 00:15:31.882
<v Alex>It turns it into an A I agent you can text, ask questions, and pay.

191
00:15:32.360 --> 00:15:38.628
<v Maya>No menus, no forms. He asks: is the best interface just a conversation?

192
00:15:39.162 --> 00:15:43.941
<v Alex>At almost the same time, a Chinese account, 关木, complains about a payment app.

193
00:15:43.941 --> 00:15:46.923
<v Alex>He just wanted to see his dentist appointment number.

194
00:15:46.923 --> 00:15:50.898
<v Alex>But the app keeps sending him to an A I chat page.

195
00:15:51.324 --> 00:15:53.237
<v Maya>Ha. I feel that.

196
00:15:53.690 --> 00:16:00.182
<v Alex>And Karri Saarinen, the founder of Linear, says more U I is being built than ever.

197
00:16:00.757 --> 00:16:06.080
<v Maya>So, a growth view. Chat helps people who don't know what they're looking for.

198
00:16:06.080 --> 00:16:09.074
<v Maya>Menus help people who know exactly what they want.

199
00:16:09.682 --> 00:16:13.353
<v Alex>For a travel site like YouChina, that's real.

200
00:16:13.353 --> 00:16:19.062
<v Alex>This one is borderline as a writing topic, unless Fisher has a real observation.

201
00:16:19.615 --> 00:16:23.990
<v Maya>So the takeaway is: don't force everyone into one path.

202
00:16:25.285 --> 00:16:29.268
<v Maya>一句话：知道要什么的人，还是需要菜单。

203
00:16:32.118 --> 00:16:34.172
<v Alex>Segment four. Quick hits.

204
00:16:34.667 --> 00:16:40.503
<v Maya>Anthropic news. Claude Code Review is now open for Team and Enterprise plans.

205
00:16:40.503 --> 00:16:45.890
<v Maya>They say most reviews finish about three times faster than in preview.

206
00:16:46.380 --> 00:16:49.049
<v Alex>Fisher doesn't write code, so skip that.

207
00:16:49.571 --> 00:16:53.969
<v Maya>Also, Opus five point five fast mode has no waitlist now.

208
00:16:53.969 --> 00:17:00.366
<v Maya>Up to two and a half times faster. But the A P I price is double.

209
00:17:00.851 --> 00:17:06.324
<v Alex>And in Claude Code, subscription users pay from extra usage when they turn on fast mode.

210
00:17:06.324 --> 00:17:08.718
<v Alex>It doesn't come from the plan.

211
00:17:09.237 --> 00:17:13.430
<v Maya>So the takeaway is: watch the bill if you turn on fast mode.

212
00:17:14.769 --> 00:17:18.343
<v Maya>一句话：快速模式要另外花钱。

213
00:17:21.193 --> 00:17:26.344
<v Alex>Borderline item. Troy Aitken says A I labs buy company data.

214
00:17:26.344 --> 00:17:29.153
<v Alex>And they want workflows, not files.

215
00:17:29.718 --> 00:17:34.499
<v Maya>Meaning the same event seen in email, chat, the C R M, and the ticket.

216
00:17:34.499 --> 00:17:38.325
<v Maya>That's worth more than a pile of records from one tool.

217
00:17:38.843 --> 00:17:43.077
<v Alex>He also says how far back the history goes matters more than size.

218
00:17:43.077 --> 00:17:47.310
<v Alex>But he works in this business, so it's a seller's view.

219
00:17:47.815 --> 00:17:56.618
<v Maya>It links to Fisher's article idea D R oh eight, about bosses building their own C R M with A I.

220
00:17:56.618 --> 00:17:59.419
<v Maya>Could be a general example. No numbers.

221
00:18:00.159 --> 00:18:06.412
<v Maya>简单说：老板自己的客户记录，正在变成能卖的资产。

222
00:18:09.262 --> 00:18:10.496
<v Alex>Last, also borderline.

223
00:18:10.496 --> 00:18:17.490
<v Alex>Alex Finn says his Grok Bot built and ran a small business this week that made money.

224
00:18:17.490 --> 00:18:20.781
<v Alex>He is giving away the template for free.

225
00:18:21.320 --> 00:18:22.415
<v Maya>Lots of saves.

226
00:18:22.415 --> 00:18:28.258
<v Maya>But one reply points out he has a big audience, so marketing is easier for him.

227
00:18:28.904 --> 00:18:33.813
<v Alex>We can't check the income claim. And it's the opposite of Fisher's rule.

228
00:18:33.813 --> 00:18:35.961
<v Alex>Fisher's bots don't send anything.

229
00:18:36.514 --> 00:18:39.381
<v Maya>So the takeaway is: skip this one.

230
00:18:40.635 --> 00:18:42.479
<v Maya>一句话：跳过。

231
00:18:45.329 --> 00:18:48.879
<v Alex>Segment five. What Fisher could post and reply today.

232
00:18:49.508 --> 00:18:55.522
<v Maya>The original post uses the memory story from the top of the show. Here is how it reads.

233
00:18:56.121 --> 00:19:01.156
<v Alex>"All my A I assistants read one memory file before they start work.

234
00:19:01.156 --> 00:19:05.804
<v Alex>Yesterday I changed where my project files live. Twice, in one morning.

235
00:19:05.804 --> 00:19:10.840
<v Alex>Each time I added the new rule. I never deleted the old one."

236
00:19:11.406 --> 00:19:18.198
<v Maya>"So now the file says two different things, and an assistant will just believe whichever one it reads first.

237
00:19:18.198 --> 00:19:23.203
<v Maya>New rule for me: when I change a rule, I delete the old line."

238
00:19:23.738 --> 00:19:26.789
<v Alex>Plain. Real. No big claims.

239
00:19:27.414 --> 00:19:32.627
<v Maya>There are ten reply drafts. The top ones: reply to klöss about memory.

240
00:19:32.627 --> 00:19:38.956
<v Maya>But pick that or the original post, not both, so the same story isn't told twice.

241
00:19:39.488 --> 00:19:43.872
<v Alex>Reply to Eric Siu: most of my limits are about doing less.

242
00:19:43.872 --> 00:19:50.082
<v Alex>Reply to Shann: a list of exact phrases works better than "don't sound like A I".

243
00:19:50.717 --> 00:19:52.662
<v Maya>One reply needs a check.

244
00:19:52.662 --> 00:20:00.054
<v Maya>For Lily Ray, the draft says part of his A I traffic showed as Direct for a few days.

245
00:20:00.054 --> 00:20:04.334
<v Maya>Fisher should confirm that's true. If not, delete that sentence.

246
00:20:04.797 --> 00:20:08.887
<v Alex>And the reply to Greg is about choosing older writing for the voice guide.

247
00:20:09.263 --> 00:20:16.868
<v Maya>今天的待办：选一条原创发，确认 Lily Ray 那条的事实，再挑几条回复。

248
00:20:17.422 --> 00:20:22.270
<v Alex>So the takeaway is: one real story beats ten hot takes.

249
00:20:23.314 --> 00:20:28.356
<v Maya>一句话：一个真实的故事，比十个热点评论有用。

250
00:20:31.206 --> 00:20:36.201
<v Alex>That's it for today. Remember, nothing was posted or sent.

251
00:20:36.201 --> 00:20:40.741
<v Alex>The posts and replies are drafts. Fisher sends them himself.

252
00:20:41.241 --> 00:20:46.163
<v Maya>And today's episode is audio only. It was not published to the feed.

253
00:20:46.588 --> 00:20:47.756
<v Alex>I'm Alex.

254
00:20:48.164 --> 00:20:49.065
<v Maya>I'm Maya.

255
00:20:49.658 --> 00:20:53.249
